Stop a request as soon as its client leaves, even while it is queued, held for memory or prefilling.
Stock 0.6.6 CUDA serving notices a closed connection only when the request's thread receives tokens, so a
request that is still waiting or prefilling (a 60K-token agent prompt takes ~2 minutes here) runs to its first
token after the client hung up: pressing Esc in pi left the GPU busy and pi's next request queued behind it.
Now the request's thread polls the socket every TENSORFOLD_CANCEL_POLL_S (0.25 s; 0 = stock) while no tokens come,
and the scheduler worker drops a gone request from the queue, the memory wait, or between prompt passes (the
MultiDecoder prefill loop also returns between passes once a filling prompt's client has gone).
A prompt cancelled mid-prefill first fills one more pass to a kept point (never inside an image), so a resend
resumes there: pi times out after 300 s and resends, and a prompt longer than that (237K tokens at ~710 tok/s)
would otherwise start over every time. Prefix states already kept stay cached. Two-rank (tp 2) decoders keep the
stock behaviour.
Verified: fake-decoder test (flash-3/tools/test_client_cancel.py) and live long prompts aborted mid-prefill.

